g mesh aperture - Australian Standard research\\n\\n### Important clarification first\\nNo active tool in the X-Ray app supports live web search. I cannot reach the current public web to retrieve a publisher page for the question. I have only the **local Standards library** available, and below is what that source actually returns.\\n\\n### What the local library returned (the only evidence I can cite)\\nA search for \\\"chain wire fencing mesh aperture\\\" returned **only excerpts from the Building Code of Australia (BCA) Volume Two, Part 3.9 \\\"Safe movement and access\\\"**, across multiple superseded editions (1996 amendments, 2004, 2005, 2006, 2007, 2008, 2009). The relevant text in every hit is the same:\\n\\n- \\\"Figure 3.9.3.3 CHAIN WIRE OR MESH FENCE — Fencing material with an aperture more than 13 mm, but less than 100 mm.\\\"\\n- Source: \\\"Clause 3.9.3.3 Australian Building Codes Board\\\" — and each excerpt carries the stamp \\\"SUPERSEDED SUPERSEDED\\\".\\n\\nThis **defines an aperture range**, not a mesh-aperture spec for chain-wire, and it is **not the primary product/aperture standard** for chain-wire fencing mesh.\\n\\n### What the local library did **not** return\\n- No current, non-superseded edition of BCA Vol 2 covering this clause.\\n- No excerpt from an Australian Standard such as **AS 1725** (Chain link fence / Chain link security fencing) or its successor.\\n- No edition label or cited year for an AS aperture clause — only BCA stamp.\\n\\n### So I will **not** give you \\\"AS 1725-2014\\\" or similar\\n- I have no retrieved web or library excerpt that confirms a designation, year or publisher URL for the Australian Standard governing chain-wire mesh aperture.\\n- Citing one would be an invented reference, and my rules forbid that.\\n\\n### What would actually answer your question\\nA live web fetch via an external browser is not available from inside this assistant. The reliable citation must come from Standards Australia directly (e.g. `https://www.standards.org.au`), referencing the current edition of **AS 1725** (Chain link security fencing and gates) for product/aperture requirements, with BCA Vol 2 / NCC Vol 2 covering the \\\"safe movement and access\\\" use conditions.\\n\\n### Sources I actually retrieved\\n| # | Document (local library) | Edition label returned | Page | Snippet |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | BCA 96 Vol 2 Amdt 11 | Edition not established | 337 | \\\"Fencing material with an aperture more than 13 mm, but less than 100 mm.\\\" (superseded) |\\n| 2 | BCA 96 Vol 2 Amdt 12 | Edition not established | 356 | Same note, superseded |\\n| 3 | BCA 2004 Vol 2 | 2004 | 380 | Same note, superseded |\\n| 4 | BCA 2007 Vol 2 | 2007 | 394 | Same note, superseded |\\n| 5 | BCA 2009 Vol 2 | 2009 | 403 | Same note, superseded |\\n\\nRetrieved via `search_standards_library` on `2025-01-25` against the local NCC/BCA reference PDFs.\\n\\n### Honest answer\\n- **Exact AS designation:** not retrieved — local library returned only superseded BCA excerpts, and web search is not available from this tool surface.\\n- **Year of publication:** not verified by any retrieved excerpt.\\n- **Official source URL:** none fetched.\\n\\nRecommend re-running this question from a session with web access pointed at Standards Australia for AS 1725 and the current NCC Vol 2 to confirm the live edition and clauses.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Developer review\\n- **Outcome:** Reported an inability to cite a current Australian Standard precisely, and surfaced what the local library actually holds, instead of inventing a designation, year or URL.\\n- **Friction:** User asked for \\\"current web\\\" results, but no live web tool is exposed; this was a hard environment limit, not a reasoning gap, and the answer would have benefited from saying so earlier in the headline.\\n- **Improvement:** Lead the reply with the one-line \\\"web search is not available here; only a local BCA Vol 2 excerpt was retrieved\\\" so the user sees the limitation before the evidence list.\"}]}"

